  {
    "userId": "6a06d147001a790ae1ce",
    "sessionId": "6a086d6100049b642f85",
    "front": "What is the relationship between a circle's radius and its circumference?",
    "back": "The circumference $C$ is directly proportional to the radius $r$ through the constant $2\\pi$: $C = 2\\pi r$. This means if you double the radius, the circumference doubles. The constant $\\pi$ (approximately 3.14159) is the ratio of any circle's circumference to its diameter, so $C = \\pi d$ is equivalent.",
    "confidence": 1,
