  {
    "userId": "6a06d147001a790ae1ce",
    "sessionId": "6a086d6100049b642f85",
    "front": "A pendulum bob swings in a circular arc of radius 1.5 m. At the bottom of its swing, its speed is 4.2 m/s and the string tension is 85 N. What is the mass of the bob?",
    "back": "At the bottom of the swing, the net centripetal force is the difference between tension (upward) and weight (downward): $F_c = T - mg = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$. Rearranging: $T = m\\left(g + \\frac{v^2}{r}\\right)$. Solve for $m$: $85 = m\\left(9.8 + \\frac{4.2^2}{1.5}\\right) = m\\left(9.8 + 11.76\\right) = m(21.56)$. So $m = \\frac{85}{21.56} \\approx 3.94$ kg. This combines circular motion mechanics with forces acting simultaneously — a common real-world scenario in pendulum clocks, amusement rides, and cable-suspended loads.",
    "confidence": 2,
