  {
    "userId": "6a06d147001a790ae1ce",
    "sessionId": "6a086d6100049b642f85",
    "front": "A 1500 kg car rounds a flat, circular curve of radius 50 m at a speed of 15 m/s. What is the minimum coefficient of static friction required between the tires and the road to prevent the car from skidding?",
    "back": "On a flat curve, friction provides the centripetal force: $f_s = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$. The maximum static friction is $f_{s,\\text{max}} = \\mu_s N = \\mu_s mg$. Setting these equal at the limit of skidding: $\\mu_s mg = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$. The mass cancels: $\\mu_s = \\frac{v^2}{rg} = \\frac{15^2}{50 \\times 9.8} = \\frac{225}{490} \\approx 0.459$. So the tires need a friction coefficient of at least 0.46. This is why icy roads (low $\\mu_s$) make curves dangerous — the required friction exceeds what the surface can provide, and the car slides outward.",
    "confidence": 3,
