  {
    "userId": "6a06d147001a790ae1ce",
    "sessionId": "6a086d6100049b642f85",
    "front": "A circular water tank has a radius of 3 m and a depth of 4 m. What is the total volume of water the tank can hold?",
    "back": "The tank is a cylinder, so volume is the circular base area times the height: $V = \\pi r^2 h = \\pi(3^2)(4) = 9\\pi \\times 4 = 36\\pi \\approx 113.1$ cubic meters. In liters (1 m³ = 1000 L), this is about 113,100 liters. The circular cross-section is optimal for pressure distribution: the curved walls experience only tensile (hoop) stress with no bending moments, allowing thinner walls than a square tank of equal volume.",
    "confidence": 3,
