    "sessionId": "6a086d6100049b642f85",
    "front": "A 0.2 kg mass on a string moves in a vertical circle of radius 0.8 m. At the top of the circle, the string tension is zero. What is the speed of the mass at the top?",
    "back": "At the top of a vertical circle, both tension and weight point downward toward the center. When tension is zero, the weight alone provides the centripetal force: $mg = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$. The mass cancels: $v = \\sqrt{rg} = \\sqrt{0.8 \\times 9.8} = \\sqrt{7.84} \\approx 2.80$ m/s. This is the minimum speed needed to complete the loop — any slower and the mass would fall before reaching the top. This principle governs roller coaster loop design and carnival rides.",
    "confidence": 1,
